  "summary": "Hand-stitched lace ruffles on tembleque combs — the dress Panamá wears for its soul.",
  "description": "The pollera — Panama's national dress — is a white cotton-and-linen gown built from two or three hand-stitched ruffle tiers (polleras, faldones) embroidered with floral motifs in silk or lace (dead, ela, mosqueta styles), worn with layered gold chains, hand-made chichería combs and the towering lace tembleque headdress. Azuero Peninsula towns like Las Tablas and Guararé keep the strictest canons: sewing a gala pollera takes a year and a small fortune, girls inherit grandmothers' sets, and the national Mejorana Festival crowns pollera queens. Its sibling, the men's montuno embroidered shirt-and-trousers, completes the picture at parades and world events.",
  "culturalContext": "The pollera is Panama's national dress and Azuero social fabric: a year's hand-stitching per gala gown, grandmothers' sets inherited like jewels, the Mejorana Festival crowning queens, and the montuno-and-pollera pair representing the nation at world events — Spanish colonial dress remade as Panamanian pride.",
